    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Left chest wall pain at the scar of an old penetrating arrow wound, with breathlessness on exertion and a reduced FVC (78 percent predicted) with a normal FEV1/FVC ratio, and heavy episodic drinking (AUDIT 19, PEth 236 ng/mL), found at intake; cause and extent under evaluation with a pulmonology consultation and an addiction medicine assessment</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R07.89</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to the documented chest wound and the documented drinking parties; superseded at the consultation, when the chest wall pain, the pleural thickening and the alcohol use disorder were diagnosed.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Chronic post-traumatic chest wall pain: intercostal neuropathic pain at the healed left chest wound and fifth rib fracture, with tenderness and a small area of allodynia over the scar</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G89.21</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the consultation, anchored to the documented arrow wound; not a claim about his real chest.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Localized left pleural thickening (fibrothorax) from an old organized hemothorax after a penetrating chest wound, with a mild restrictive defect (TLC 79 percent predicted) and normal gas transfer; no retained foreign body, fistula or trapped lung on CT</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J94.1</td><td>S — Synthetic modern finding of the CT and lung function tests at the consultation, anchored to the documented arrow wound; the retrospective pneumothorax (CLM-0004) is not used.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Alcohol use disorder, mild (three DSM-5-TR criteria): drinking more than intended at banquets, riding after drinking, and arguments with his officers while drunk; ten or more drinks on two or three nights a week, more since his bereavement; no withdrawal; AUDIT 19; PEth 236 ng/mL</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F10.10</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made with the addiction medicine clinician at the consultation visit, anchored to the documented drinking parties; the criteria, amounts and scores are synthetic. The diagnosis is mild, in keeping with the disputed evidence (CLM-0008).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Bereavement five months after the death of his closest companion since boyhood, with depressive symptoms (PHQ-9 12, item 9 scored 0) that do not meet criteria for a major depressive episode; prolonged grief disorder cannot be diagnosed before twelve months</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z63.4</td><td>S — Synthetic modern assessment anchored to the documented bereavement; DSM-5-TR codes uncomplicated bereavement as Z63.4 whatever the relationship. Not a claim about his real mental health.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-6">
    <td>Laceration of the left forearm, 4 cm, from a practice blade in a training drill; clean edges, no tendon, nerve or vessel injury; repaired with sutures; healed</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>S51.812A</td><td>S — Synthetic acute injury; not a historical claim.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

